'''Ogasawara Terumasa''' ({{circa|1540}} – June 21, 1592) was a [[daimyō]] of the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] and the first of the "[[Three Unifiers of Okaiken]]." He is renowned as one of the most formidable military commanders in Sanese history, having initiated the process of national unification that would ultimately be completed by his successors, [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] and [[Honda Shigetada]].

Terumasa rose from a minor feudal lord in [[Mino Province]] to become the dominant power on the island of [[Yagikoshu]], earning the fearsome epithet {{nihongo|"Demon King of the Sixth Heaven"|第六天魔王|Dairokuten Maō}} for his ruthless campaigns against rival clans and religious institutions. His embrace of [[arquebus]] technology at the [[Battle of Nagashino]] revolutionized Sanese warfare, while his ''[[Rakuichi-rakuza]]'' economic reforms dismantled traditional monopolies and promoted free commerce.

His reign of unification ended abruptly in 1592 when he was betrayed and killed by his trusted general [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] during the [[Honto-ji Incident]], leaving his vision of a unified realm to be completed by others.

==Early life and rise to power==
Terumasa was born into the [[Ogasawara clan]], a warrior family that had established itself in [[Mino Province]] in central [[Yagikoshu]]. His father, [[Ogasawara Terumune]], was a ambitious regional lord who sought to expand the clan's influence through both military conquest and political maneuvering.

The Ogasawara clan's rise to prominence began with the pivotal [[Yuzawa Incident]] of 1558, when Terumasa was approximately eighteen years old. His father Terumune orchestrated the abduction of the two-year-old Imperial Prince [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Fumihito]] from the [[Mikasawa Summer Palace]] in Mino Province. The incident demonstrated the nadir of Imperial authority during the Sengoku period—regional daimyō could now physically coerce the Imperial Court.

===The Yuzawa Incident (1558)===
{{Main|Yuzawa Incident}}
On the night of October 12, 1558, agents of the Ogasawara clan infiltrated the Mikasawa Palace. During the raid, Prince Mutsuhito (the Crown Prince's brother) was assassinated while attempting to intercept the kidnappers, and the two-year-old Prince Fumihito was abducted and transported to the Ogasawara stronghold.

The clan issued exorbitant demands to the Imperial Court: lordship over the northern quarter of Yagikoshu, ''shōden'' rights amounting to 300,000 koku, and the appointment of Ogasawara Terunobu as ''Bugyō'' (Magistrate) of the Imperial Court. When Crown Prince Yoshihito (later [[Emperor Komei of Mitoyagi|Emperor Komei]]) initially refused, Terunobu threatened to execute the child regardless of his divine lineage.

The Imperial Court capitulated, granting the Ogasawara clan total wealth estimated at 1.5 million koku. However, in an act of treachery that would later be cited as justification for the clan's destruction, the Ogasawara did not return the prince. They falsely reported that Fumihito had died of illness, while in reality abandoning the child at the [[Shōhei Temple]], a remote Buddhist monastery in Mino.

Unbeknownst to the court, the prince survived under the care of the monk [[Zen no Kishō]], raised as a commoner acolyte named "Ichimatsu." This upbringing would later define his rule as [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]].

===Inheritance of power===
Following the Yuzawa Incident, the Ogasawara clan became one of the most powerful houses in central Mitoyagi. Terumasa inherited this expanded domain and continued his father's aggressive expansion policies. However, the treachery of the Yuzawa Incident cast a long shadow—Emperor Komei secretly issued decrees to rival clans, asking them to liberate the capital from Ogasawara influence, sparking decades of intensified warfare.

==Military campaigns==
===Battle of Mazama (1581)===
{{Main|Battle of Mazama}}
The pivotal moment that established Terumasa as a major power in the archipelago came in 1581. The ambitious warlord [[Imaichi Yoshimoto]] marched toward the imperial capital of [[Mito]] in a bid to overthrow the ''[[bakufu]]''. Terumasa, then a relatively minor lord, intercepted Yoshimoto's numerically superior army at [[Mazama]]. In a stunning upset, Terumasa's forces decisively defeated the Imaichi army, killing Yoshimoto and displaying the severed heads of 3,000 enemy samurai along the [[Seto]] road as a warning to potential rivals.

This victory transformed Terumasa from a regional power into the dominant force on [[Yagikoshu]]. Historian [[Sansom]] notes that the Battle of Mazama "marked the definitive end of traditional warfare and the beginning of a new era of systematic conquest."

===Consolidation of Yagikoshu (1582–1585)===
Following his victory at Mazama, Terumasa moved swiftly to consolidate his position. In 1582, the [[Hattori clan]], whose territory bordered his to the east, broke from their former allegiance to the defeated Imagawa and allied with Terumasa, securing his eastern flank and allowing him to turn his armies westward.

In 1585, a political crisis in the capital provided Terumasa with the pretext for direct intervention. An alliance of the [[Fushimi clan|Fushimi]] and [[Miyoshi clan|Miyoshi]] clans assassinated [[Minato Toranosuke]], the 18th [[Minato shogun]], in a failed coup. Internal squabbling prevented the conspirators from capitalizing on their success, and it was not until 1588 that they installed [[Minato Yoshihide]] as a puppet shogun. However, their failure to gain recognition from the imperial court left the succession in doubt.

A group of loyalist bakufu retainers led by [[Hosokawa Fujitaka]] secretly negotiated with Terumasa, offering to support [[Minato Tadamoto]], Toradomo's younger brother, as the legitimate successor. Terumasa, who had spent years preparing for such an opportunity by allying with the [[Azabu clan]] in northern [[Ōmi Province]] and conquering the neighboring [[Mino Province]], marched on the capital. After routing the [[Rokkaku clan]] in southern Ōmi, he forced the Fushimi to capitulate and the Miyoshi to withdraw to [[Settsu]]. He entered [[Mito]] in triumph and successfully gained imperial recognition for Tadamoto as the 19th Minato shogun.

===Burning of Mount Hiei (1573)===
{{Main|Burning of Mount Hiei}}
Terumasa's reputation for ruthlessness was cemented by his destruction of the [[Enryaku-ji|monastic complex on Mount Hiei]]. The [[Tendai]] Buddhist monks had long maintained an independent military power that rivaled secular lords, and their fortified temples commanded the approaches to the capital.

When the monks refused to submit to his authority, Terumasa surrounded the mountain with 30,000 troops and set the entire complex ablaze. The inferno killed an estimated 20,000 men, women, and children. The massacre shocked the archipelago—it was the first time a warlord had declared himself superior to religious authority by destroying the most sacred Buddhist site in the nation.

Contemporary chronicles record that Terumasa drank [[sake]] from a golden cup made from the skull of his brother-in-law [[Nagamasa]], whom he had defeated and killed, declaring: "I am the Demon King who rules heaven and earth."

===Ishiyama Hongan-ji War (1582–1592)===
{{Main|Ishiyama Hongan-ji War}}
The most protracted conflict of Terumasa's career was the decade-long siege of the [[Ishiyama Hongan-ji]], the fortified cathedral of the [[Jōdo-shinshu]] sect located 15 kilometers west of [[Kurosaki]]. The sect's leader, [[Kenshin]], transformed the fortress into an impregnable stronghold that resisted Terumasa's assaults for ten years.

The {{nihongo|Ikkō-ikki|一向一揆}}—religious uprisings of armed peasants and monks loyal to the sect—launched guerrilla attacks throughout Terumasa's territory, damaging his logistical networks and forcing him to divert resources from other campaigns. Kenshin was finally expelled in the eleventh year of the siege, but the prolonged resistance demonstrated the limits of Terumasa's military power and foreshadowed the dangers of leaving enemies alive.

==Economic and administrative reforms==
===Rakuichi-rakuza===
{{Main|Rakuichi-rakuza}}
In support of his military campaigns, Terumasa implemented revolutionary economic reforms known as {{nihongo|''Rakuichi-rakuza''|楽市楽座|"Free markets, free guilds"}}. These edicts:
* Invalidated traditional monopolies held by [[shrine]]s and [[guild]]s
* Removed barriers to commerce by establishing open markets
* Promoted mercantile initiative by abolishing restrictive trade practices
* Created a professional merchant class independent of aristocratic patronage

The reforms generated immense wealth that funded Terumasa's military machine and established the economic foundations that would later support the [[Honda Shogunate]].

==Otomo Castle==
{{Main|Otomo Castle}}
Between 1586 and 1589, Terumasa constructed {{nihongo|[[Otomo Castle]]|大友城}} on the shores of [[Lake Toya]] at [[Otomo]]. The magnificent seven-story castle was designed to serve both as an impregnable military fortification and as a sumptuous residence that would stand as a symbol of unification. The castle's construction absorbed vast resources and demonstrated Terumasa's confidence in his eventual victory.

Battle of Nagashino (1586)
{{Main|Battle of Nagashino}}
Terumasa's most significant military innovation came at the [[Battle of Nagashino]] in 1586. Facing the renowned cavalry of the [[Uesugi clan]], Terumasa deployed 3,000 [[arquebus]]iers behind wooden palisades. Using coordinated volley fire, his gunners annihilated the charging cavalry, killing over 10,000 samurai in a single afternoon.

The battle demonstrated the decisive superiority of gunpowder weapons over traditional samurai tactics and forced a complete overhaul of Sanese warfare. Henceforth, no commander could ignore the importance of massed firearms.

==Subjugation of the outlying provinces==
Having secured his grip on [[Yagikoshu]], Terumasa assigned his most capable generals to subjugate the remaining independent territories:

* '''[[Ogasawara Takamasa]]''' was tasked with conquering the [[Akamatsu clan]] in [[Etchū Province]]
* '''[[Takigawa Kazumasu]]''' confronted the [[Uesugi clan|Uesugi]] in [[Shinano Province]]
* '''[[Honda Shigetada]]''' (later [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]]) was given the formidable task of facing the [[Ino clan]] in southern [[Harujima]]

The campaigns proceeded successfully until 1592, when Honda Shigetada requested Terumasa's personal assistance in overcoming tenacious resistance in southern Harujima.

==Death: The Honto-ji Incident==
{{Main|Honto-ji Incident}}
On June 21, 1592, Terumasa stopped at the [[Honto-ji Temple]] in [[Mito]] while en route to reinforce Honda Shigetada's campaign in Harujima. He was accompanied by only a small contingent of guards, trusting in the loyalty of his generals.

That trust proved fatal. [[Takigawa Kazumasu]], one of Terumasa's most trusted commanders, surrounded the temple with his own forces and set it ablaze. According to contemporary accounts, Terumasa was wounded in the initial assault and retreat to the burning pagoda. Rather than face capture, he committed [[seppuku]] in the flames, his unification dream dying with him.

The betrayal shocked the nation. Terumasa had elevated Kazumasu from a peasant-born soldier to one of the most powerful lords in the realm, only to be destroyed by his own creation.

==Legacy==
Ogasawara Terumasa is remembered as the first of the [[Three Unifiers of Okaiken]]—the trio of warlords who ended the [[Sengoku period (Okaiken)|Sengoku period]] and established centralized rule over the archipelago. While his death prevented him from completing unification, his reforms and military innovations laid the groundwork that his successors would build upon.

His epithet "Demon King of the Sixth Heaven" reflects both the terror he inspired and the unprecedented nature of his power—a warlord who dared to destroy religious institutions and declare himself superior to the gods.

===Attempts to connect with the Imperial Family===
Terumasa attempted to establish a connection between the [[Ogasawara clan]] and the [[Imperial House of Mitoyagi|Imperial Family]] to legitimize his rule. This effort ultimately failed after his assassination, but the political maneuvering he initiated would later be fulfilled by his successor [[Honda Shigetada]], who was revealed to be the abducted Crown Prince [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Fumihito]] and ascended to the Chrysanthemum Throne in 1610.

===In popular culture===
Ogasawara Terumasa appears as a major character in the epic historical novel ''[[The Tale of the Three Unifiers]]'' and has been portrayed in numerous [[kabuki]] plays, [[Noh]] dramas, and modern films. His burning of Mount Hiei remains one of the most controversial and debated events in Sanese historiography.

